## 8. StaffHub Practical 1 — Email Update

The StaffHub lab demonstrated an email-update form.

### Original Form

```html
<form action="update_email.php" method="POST">
    <input type="email" name="email" required>
    <button type="submit">Update Email</button>
</form>
```

### Important Form Attributes

| Attribute | Description |
|---|---|
| `action` | Specifies the endpoint receiving the form submission |
| `method="POST"` | Specifies the HTTP POST method |
| `name="email"` | Defines the form parameter name |
| `required` | Requires the field to contain a value before submission |

### Lab Observation

The application did not adequately verify the legitimacy of the email-update request.

The lab demonstrated how a crafted form could attempt to trigger an email-update request through the authenticated browser context.

The AttackBox example used a hidden form field containing an email value:

```html
<input type="hidden" name="email"
       value="attacker@evilmail.thm">
```

The following JavaScript statement submits a form programmatically:

```javascript
document.getElementById("attack").submit();
```

The room's lab hint identified `special@evilmail.thm` as the expected email value for completing the task.

**Key Learning:** Sensitive account changes must be protected by server-side request verification, not merely by the presence of a logged-in session.

