## 12. CSRF vs. XSS

| CSRF | XSS |
|---|---|
| Attempts to make an authenticated browser perform an unwanted request | Executes malicious script in a webpage's context |
| Abuses insufficient request verification and authenticated browser behavior | Commonly results from unsafe handling of untrusted content |
| Can affect account settings and other state-changing actions | Can affect page content, user interactions, and application data accessible to the script |
| Defenses include CSRF tokens, SameSite cookies, and request validation | Defenses include output encoding, safe DOM APIs, and Content Security Policy |

**Key Difference:** CSRF focuses on unauthorized actions through a victim's authenticated browser, whereas XSS involves script execution in a webpage's context.

---

## 13. Important Terminology

| Term | Definition |
|---|---|
| CSRF | Cross-Site Request Forgery |
| Session Cookie | A cookie used to help identify a user's session |
| State-Changing Request | A request that modifies application data or settings |
| CSRF Token | An unpredictable value used to verify sensitive requests |
| Base64 | An encoding scheme, not encryption |
| Forged Request | A request crafted to attempt an unintended action |
| Origin | The combination of scheme, host, and port identifying a web origin |
| SameSite | A cookie attribute that controls cookie behavior in cross-site contexts |

